From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ghostframe <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 09:32:14 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] v4l2: report a stalled decode instead of panicking

`DeviceHandle::sync` gives a queued request ~250 ms of exponential back-off
and then

    panic!("there should not be a scenario where a queued frame is not returned.")

There is such a scenario: a driver that does not return the buffer, or a
bitstream the hardware chokes on. Neither is a programming error in this
crate, and the caller is typically a render thread, where a panic takes the
whole session down over one bad frame.

The error already had somewhere to go -- `DecodedHandle::sync` returns
`anyhow::Result<()>` and was discarding it with `Ok(())`. This threads the
failure out to it:

    DeviceHandle::sync      -> Result<V4l2CaptureBuffer<V>, QueueError>
    V4l2Device::sync        -> Result<..., QueueError>
    PendingRequestHandle    -> Result<DoneRequestHandle<V>, QueueError>
    RequestHandle::sync     -> Result<(), QueueError>
    V4l2Request::sync       -> Result<(), QueueError>
    V4l2Picture::sync       -> anyhow::Result<()>
    DecodedHandle::sync     -> unchanged signature, now returns the error

`PendingRequestHandle::sync` takes `&self` rather than `self` so a failure
leaves the request Pending instead of destroying it, which also lets
`RequestHandle::sync` drop its `std::mem::take` and stop passing through the
`Unknown` state on every call. An out-of-order `sync` on an `Init` request is
now `QueueError::State` rather than `panic!("ERROR")`.

`RequestHandle::result` still panics when called before `sync`; that is API
misuse rather than a driver condition, and `DecodedHandle::video_frame` has no
`Result` to carry it. Left as-is deliberately.
